-- Boundary module: the Acts Interpretation Act 1901 (Cth) provisions on time that the
-- Diplomatic Appointments (Selection Process) Bill 2026 reads.
-- Source: Acts Interpretation Act 1901, Compilation No. 39, compilation date 28 March 2026,
-- register ID C2026C00117 (0H item cth-acts-interpretation-act-1901).
-- Reference register: REF-01 (ss 3, 3A), REF-09 (ss 36, 37), REF-20 (ss 2M, 36),
-- REF-22 (s 36), REF-23 (ss 2G, 36), REF-31 (ss 3A, 2G, 36).
--
-- Two byte-identical copies of this file exist: aia-time.l4 in the subject folder, which the Bill's
-- modules import, and boundary/aia-time.l4, the path the reference register names. The installed l4
-- cannot import from another folder (ENCODING-NOTES.md, E-01).
--
-- Dates are day numbers: day 0 is 1 January 1970, and each later day is one more. The
-- installed l4's daydate library provides nothing, so the calendar is built here by hand
-- (proleptic Gregorian calendar; the civil calendar, not a provision of any Act).
-- A day is a whole day. The Bill's periods are counted in days, months, years and sitting
-- days, never in hours, so the time of day at which a thing happened is not modelled
-- (s 37, legal time in each State, therefore decides nothing here).

§ `The civil calendar (no provision)`

DECLARE `civil date` HAS
    `year`         IS A NUMBER
    `month`        IS A NUMBER
    `day of month` IS A NUMBER

-- The day number of a civil date (days from 1 January 1970).
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the day` y m d MEANS
    (era TIMES 146097) PLUS doe MINUS 719468
    WHERE
        yy  MEANS IF m AT MOST 2 THEN y MINUS 1 ELSE y
        era MEANS FLOOR (yy DIVIDED BY 400)
        yoe MEANS yy MINUS (era TIMES 400)
        mp  MEANS IF m GREATER THAN 2 THEN m MINUS 3 ELSE m PLUS 9
        doy MEANS FLOOR (((153 TIMES mp) PLUS 2) DIVIDED BY 5) PLUS d MINUS 1
        doe MEANS (yoe TIMES 365) PLUS FLOOR (yoe DIVIDED BY 4) MINUS FLOOR (yoe DIVIDED BY 100) PLUS doy

-- The civil date of a day number.
GIVEN z IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `civil date`
`the civil date of` z MEANS
    `civil date` WITH
        `year`         IS (IF mm AT MOST 2 THEN yy PLUS 1 ELSE yy)
        `month`        IS mm
        `day of month` IS dd
    WHERE
        zz  MEANS z PLUS 719468
        era MEANS FLOOR (zz DIVIDED BY 146097)
        doe MEANS zz MINUS (era TIMES 146097)
        yoe MEANS FLOOR ((doe MINUS FLOOR (doe DIVIDED BY 1460) PLUS FLOOR (doe DIVIDED BY 36524) MINUS FLOOR (doe DIVIDED BY 146096)) DIVIDED BY 365)
        yy  MEANS yoe PLUS (era TIMES 400)
        doy MEANS doe MINUS ((365 TIMES yoe) PLUS FLOOR (yoe DIVIDED BY 4) MINUS FLOOR (yoe DIVIDED BY 100))
        mp  MEANS FLOOR (((5 TIMES doy) PLUS 2) DIVIDED BY 153)
        dd  MEANS doy MINUS FLOOR (((153 TIMES mp) PLUS 2) DIVIDED BY 5) PLUS 1
        mm  MEANS IF mp LESS THAN 10 THEN mp PLUS 3 ELSE mp MINUS 9

-- The first day of the calendar month that is n calendar months after the month of (y, m).
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the first day of the month` n `months after the month of` y m MEANS
    `the day` (y PLUS FLOOR (t DIVIDED BY 12)) ((t MODULO 12) PLUS 1) 1
    WHERE
        t MEANS (m MINUS 1) PLUS n

-- How many days the calendar month that is n months after the month of (y, m) has.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the length of the month` n `months after the month of` y m MEANS
        `the first day of the month` (n PLUS 1) `months after the month of` y m
    MINUS `the first day of the month` n `months after the month of` y m

-- The day with the same month and day of month n calendar years before day z; where that year
-- has no such day (29 February), the next day, 1 March. The Acts Interpretation Act defines no
-- "year", so this is the calendar, not a provision (ENCODING-NOTES.md, E-05).
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, z IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the same date` n `years before` z MEANS
    IF     (c's `day of month`) AT MOST `the length of the month` 0 `months after the month of` ((c's `year`) MINUS n) (c's `month`)
    THEN   `the day` ((c's `year`) MINUS n) (c's `month`) (c's `day of month`)
    ELSE   `the first day of the month` 1 `months after the month of` ((c's `year`) MINUS n) (c's `month`)
    WHERE
        c MEANS `the civil date of` z

-- The day of the week: 0 Sunday, 1 Monday, ..., 6 Saturday. 1 January 1970 was a Thursday.
GIVEN z IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the weekday of` z MEANS (z PLUS 4) MODULO 7

GIVEN z IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a Saturday or a Sunday` z IF
        `the weekday of` z EQUALS 0
     OR `the weekday of` z EQUALS 6

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER, xs IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
x `is listed in` xs MEANS
    CONSIDER xs
    WHEN EMPTY THEN FALSE
    WHEN y FOLLOWED BY rest THEN (y EQUALS x) OR (x `is listed in` rest)

§ `Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 2G -- Months`

-- s 2G(1): "month means a period: (a) starting at the start of any day of one of the calendar
-- months; and (b) ending: (i) immediately before the start of the corresponding day of the next
-- calendar month; or (ii) if there is no such day--at the end of the next calendar month."
-- s 2G(2): "a reference to a period of 2 or more months is a reference to a period: (a) starting
-- at the start of a day of one of the calendar months (the starting month); and (b) ending:
-- (i) immediately before the start of the corresponding day of the calendar month that is that
-- number of calendar months after the starting month; or (ii) if there is no such day--at the
-- end of the calendar month that is that number of calendar months after the starting month."
-- Both subsections give the same answer for n = 1, so one rule serves both.
--
-- The first day AFTER a period of n months that starts at the start of day s: the corresponding
-- day where there is one (s 2G(2)(b)(i)); otherwise the first day of the month after the
-- n-th month, because the period ends at the end of the n-th month (s 2G(2)(b)(ii)).
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 2G
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, s IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the first day after a period of` n `months starting on` s MEANS
    IF     (c's `day of month`) AT MOST `the length of the month` n `months after the month of` (c's `year`) (c's `month`)
    THEN   `the first day of the month` n `months after the month of` (c's `year`) (c's `month`) PLUS (c's `day of month`) MINUS 1
    ELSE   `the first day of the month` (n PLUS 1) `months after the month of` (c's `year`) (c's `month`)
    WHERE
        c MEANS `the civil date of` s

-- The last day of a period of n months starting on day s.
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 2G
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, s IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the last day of a period of` n `months starting on` s MEANS
    `the first day after a period of` n `months starting on` s MINUS 1

§ `Acts Interpretation Act 1901 ss 3 and 3A -- Commencement`

-- s 3: "If an Act or a provision of an Act is expressed to come into operation on a particular
-- day ... it comes into operation at the start of the day."
-- s 3A(1): "This section does not apply to an Act so far as it provides for its commencement."
-- The Bill provides for its own commencement (s 2(1), item 1), so s 3A(2) (the 28th day after
-- Royal Assent) does not apply; s 3 puts commencement at the start of the day s 2 names.
-- A day number stands for the whole day, so "at the start of the day" is that day number.
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 3
GIVEN d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the moment of operation of an Act expressed to commence on` d MEANS d

-- s 3A(2), for completeness of the boundary only: an Act that does not provide for its
-- commencement commences on the 28th day after Royal Assent. Not used by the Bill (s 3A(1)).
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 3A(2)
GIVEN assent IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the default commencement day for an Act assented to on` assent MEANS assent PLUS 28

§ `Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 36 -- Calculating time`

-- s 36(1), table item 6: a period "expressed to begin after a specified day ... does not include
-- that day". Item 5 (begin from a specified day) is the same. So such a period's first day is
-- the next day.
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 36(1) items 5 and 6
GIVEN d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the first day of a period beginning after` d MEANS d PLUS 1

-- s 36(1), table item 2: a period "expressed to begin at, on or with a specified day ...
-- includes that day".
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 36(1) item 2
GIVEN d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the first day of a period beginning on` d MEANS d

-- s 36(2): "If: (a) an Act requires or allows a thing to be done; and (b) the last day for doing
-- the thing is a Saturday, a Sunday or a holiday; then the thing may be done on the next day
-- that is not a Saturday, a Sunday or a holiday."
-- s 36(3): holiday means a public holiday in the place where the thing is to be done, or a day
-- the office where it is to be done is closed for the whole day. Which days those are is a fact:
-- the caller supplies them as a list of day numbers.
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 36(2), (3)
GIVEN last IS A NUMBER, holidays IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the last day for doing a thing, given s 36(2), when the stated last day is` last `and the holidays are` holidays MEANS
    IF   `is a Saturday or a Sunday` last OR (last `is listed in` holidays)
    THEN `the last day for doing a thing, given s 36(2), when the stated last day is` (last PLUS 1) `and the holidays are` holidays
    ELSE last

§ `Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 2M -- Sitting day`

-- s 2M(1): "a sitting day, in relation to a House of the Parliament, is a day on which the House
-- actually sits." s 2M(2): a sitting that continues, without adjourning, onto a later day is part
-- of the earlier sitting day. Which days a House sat is a fact (the Journals of each House); the
-- caller supplies them as a list of day numbers, already reduced under s 2M(2), so a sitting
-- carried past midnight appears once, on the day it began.
-- How many sitting days of a House fall after day a and on or before day b.
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 2M
GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER, sittings IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of sitting days after` a `up to and including` b `among` sittings MEANS
    CONSIDER sittings
    WHEN EMPTY THEN 0
    WHEN s FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        (IF s GREATER THAN a AND s AT MOST b THEN 1 ELSE 0)
        PLUS `the number of sitting days after` a `up to and including` b `among` rest
